  <section id="faq" class="sdr-faq-section">
    <div class="container-wide">
      <div class="section-label">Outsourced SDR FAQ</div>
      <h2>Questions B2B companies ask before outsourcing sales development.</h2>
      <div class="sdr-faq-list">
        <details><summary>What is an outsourced SDR service?</summary><p>An outsourced SDR service runs part or all of a company's sales development work as a managed service. The work can include target-account research, list building, outbound calling, follow-up, response handling, qualification, meeting scheduling, CRM updates, and campaign reporting.</p></details>
        <details><summary>What does SDR mean?</summary><p>SDR means sales development representative. An SDR focuses on the work before a full sales opportunity, including prospecting, outreach, qualification, follow-up, and arranging the next conversation for a closer or account executive.</p></details>
        <details><summary>What is the difference between an SDR and a BDR?</summary><p>Companies use the titles differently. SDR often refers to early-stage sales development across inbound or outbound leads. BDR often refers more specifically to new-business outbound prospecting. In practice, the responsibilities can overlap. CallTeam scopes the actual work instead of relying on the title.</p></details>
        <details><summary>How are outsourced SDR services different from appointment setting?</summary><p>Appointment setting focuses on earning and scheduling qualified sales meetings. An outsourced SDR service is the broader operating function around that outcome, including targeting, research, outreach, follow-up, qualification, workflow, reporting, and continuous campaign improvement.</p></details>
        <details><summary>Who manages the outsourced SDR work?</summary><p>CallTeam manages the agreed campaign execution, workflow, follow-up, qualification, reporting, and improvement. The client provides offer knowledge, proof, approvals, sales availability, and ownership after a qualified handoff.</p></details>
        <details><summary>Does CallTeam provide the prospect list?</summary><p>CallTeam can build or refine a targeted B2B prospect list, or work with a list, CRM, target-account set, event list, inbound source, or dormant opportunity pool supplied by the client.</p></details>
        <details><summary>Do outsourced SDRs cold call?</summary><p>Cold calling can be a central part of the work. CallTeam is calling-led because live conversations reveal fit, objections, timing, referrals, buyer language, and next-step potential that databases cannot confirm.</p></details>
        <details><summary>Does CallTeam also use email?</summary><p>Depending on the engagement, CallTeam can connect calling with approved email follow-up and other agreed channels. The exact channel mix, infrastructure, permissions, and operating boundaries are defined before launch.</p></details>
        <details><summary>Can CallTeam work inside our CRM?</summary><p>CallTeam can work with an agreed CRM or campaign workflow when access, permissions, fields, and operating rules are clear. If direct access is not appropriate, we can use another structured reporting and handoff process.</p></details>
        <details><summary>Who approves the messaging and talk track?</summary><p>The client approves the core positioning, claims, proof, talk track, qualification questions, meeting standard, and important boundaries before launch. Real conversations can then guide agreed improvements.</p></details>
        <details><summary>How does CallTeam qualify a meeting?</summary><p>The criteria are agreed before launch. Depending on the offer, they can include account fit, role relevance, business reason, interest, timing, current approach, decision path, and a clear purpose for the next conversation.</p></details>
        <details><summary>What reporting do we receive?</summary><p>Reporting is matched to the engagement and can include activity, contact outcomes, calls, responses, referrals, objections, follow-up, qualification, meetings, segment performance, and market feedback.</p></details>
        <details><summary>How much do outsourced SDR services cost?</summary><p>Pricing depends on the market, list and research requirements, buyer seniority, outreach channels, calling and follow-up workload, qualification depth, CRM workflow, reporting, and management required. CallTeam reviews the campaign before recommending a scope.</p></details>
        <details><summary>When should a company outsource sales development?</summary><p>Outsourcing can make sense when a company needs more consistent prospecting capacity, wants to protect closers from front-end work, needs to test a new market, lacks internal SDR management, or wants a managed function without immediately adding permanent headcount.</p></details>
        <details><summary>When should a company build an internal SDR team instead?</summary><p>An internal team can make more sense when the company has a proven playbook, dedicated SDR leadership, enough scale to support the function, and a product that requires deep ongoing internal specialization.</p></details>
        <details><summary>How do we start?</summary><p>Start with a strategy call. CallTeam reviews the offer, market, target accounts, data, buyer roles, sales process, qualification standard, systems, and goals before recommending the right operating model.</p></details>
